The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
struct cgroup_file_ctx *ctx = of->priv; // unsynchronized read of priv before lock
</text>
<text>
cgroup_kn_unlock(of->kn); // releases the lock too early, before using ctx
</text>
<text>
if (ctx->psi.trigger) { ... } // use-after-free if ctx was freed between the read and this access
</text>
Reason:
The race occurs because `pressure_write()` reads `of->priv` into `ctx` without any locking, then later releases the cgroup live lock (`cgroup_kn_unlock`) before using `ctx`. Meanwhile, another CPU can call `cgroup_file_release()` (e.g., during cgroup removal) which frees `ctx` and sets `of->priv = NULL`. After the lock is released, the first CPU accesses `ctx->psi.trigger`, which is a use-after-free or NULL dereference. The explicit `cgroup_get/put` outside the lock does not prevent the race because the release can happen after the get but before the lock is re-acquired (or after the unlock).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
cgrp = cgroup_kn_lock_live(of->kn, false);
if (!cgrp)
    return -ENODEV;
ctx = of->priv; // read priv only after acquiring the live lock
if (!ctx) {
    ret = -ENODEV;
    goto out_unlock;
}
</text>
<text>
// all uses of ctx are now under the lock, and no early unlock
if (ctx->psi.trigger) {
    ret = -EBUSY;
    goto out_unlock;
}
...
out_unlock:
    cgroup_kn_unlock(of->kn);
    if (ret)
        return ret;
    return nbytes;
</text>
Reason:
The fix moves the retrieval of `ctx` (`of->priv`) to after `cgroup_kn_lock_live()` has been successfully acquired. The cgroup live lock serializes with `cgroup_file_release()` (which is called under the same lock), ensuring that if the file has been released, `of->priv` will be NULL. A check for NULL immediately after `cgroup_kn_lock_live()` catches the case where the file was destroyed. By keeping all accesses to `ctx` under the scope of the live lock until the final unlock, the race is eliminated. The explicit `cgroup_get/put` calls are removed because acquiring/releasing the live lock inherently does the reference counting.

